## Symptom
Peer `claude-quip-neural-57cb` renamed self to `amaroo-coord`, but continues to show up as `claude-quip-neural-57cb` (list / whoami / addressing / room membership still keyed to the old alias).

Live report (2026-07-12): operator observed rename did not propagate; identity remains the auto-gen `claude-…` alias.

## Expected (pick one and enforce)
Either:
1. **Disallow renaming** — refuse `register`/`init --alias` changes that would replace an existing live session's alias; tell the agent the alias is sticky for this session_id and how to start fresh if they truly need a new name; or
2. **Rename must update everywhere** — changing alias for a session_id must atomically update registry, default-session/statefile, room memberships, relay registration (if any), archives/addressing indexes, and any cached list views so peers see the new alias immediately.

Half-renames (local belief ≠ broker identity) are worse than either strict policy.

## Likely investigation pointers
- B046 session_id→alias reuse on `c2c init` / re-register may ignore or fight an explicit `--alias` change.
- Room membership / history keyed by alias string rather than session_id.
- Relay alias lease vs local broker alias divergence.
- `c2c list` / MCP list reading stale registration fields.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Documented policy: rename forbidden XOR rename is a first-class, consistent operation.
- [ ] If forbidden: clear error when agent attempts rename; old alias remains authoritative.
- [ ] If allowed: after rename, `whoami`, `list`, room membership, and send targeting all use the new alias; no dual identity for the same session_id.
- [ ] Regression test covering the chosen policy.

## Discovery
Max report 2026-07-12 while waiting on B133/B134 follow-up: `claude-quip-neural-57cb` → `amaroo-coord` rename did not stick.